<!--Post: 1815--><!--Time: 1102920983-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1815"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Josef_carlin<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Josef_carlin<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 12, 2004 - 10:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The letter sent by BTK, postmarked &#34;17 MAR 2004&#34;, to the Wichita Eagle and Beacon, was mailed in an envelope which contained the return name and address: <BR> <BR>Bill Thomas Killman <BR>1684 S. Oldmanor <BR>Wichita, KS 67218 <BR> <BR>This return address was discovered to be an empty apartment. No one with that name and connected with this case was ever identified. I propose that &#34;Bill Thomas Killman&#34; is an intended and possibly important clue in the form of a anagram &#40;a word or phrase formed by reordering the letters of another word or phrase, i.e. satin to stain&#41; such that when the letters are rearranged, reads: &#34;HL, I am Olan Mills, BTK&#34; or &#34;HL, I&#39;m a Olan Mills, BTK.&#34; I&#39;ve added the punctuation to make the sentence clear. I also hope to make its meaning and any significance clear as well. Before jumping to any conclusions, please read further. <BR> <BR>Who is BTK? BTK is a serial killer who is believed to be responsible for a string of murders between January 1974 and March 1986 in Wichita, Kansas. Over the years he has written several letters to Wichita police and to local Wichita media which contain details of the crimes only the killer would know. These letters and parcels have contained photographs, diagrams and drawings of the crime scene, and most recently photocopies of word puzzles. Profiles by the police and others mention that he has a interest in photography and the ability to process photographs. It has been reported that Wichita police obtained a list of Wichita area subscribers of &#34;Games&#34; magazine in their efforts to develop a suspect list. In one of his earliest correspondences he wrote, &#34;The code words for me will be... Bind them, toture &#40;sic&#41; them, kill them. B.T.K.&#34;. <BR> <BR>Who is Olan Mills? Olan Mills is a national photographic studio chain that over the years has had multiple locations in Wichita. In the mid 70&#39;s they had a Wichita location at 4100 E. Harry. This is just three-quarters of a mile from the Nancy Fox murder at 843 S. Pershing, one and one-half miles from the 600 S. Pinecrest attempt, and two miles from the Shirley Vian murder at 1311 S. Hydraulic. In the mid 80&#39;s they had a studio at 601 N. West Street, just two miles from the Vicki Wegerle murder at 2404 W.13th.  At least one picture published of the victims was a portrait taken by this studio. These four locations are just 3-5 minutes away from Olan Mills studio&#39;s and include four of the six crime scenes. <BR> <BR>Who is HL? On January 17, 2004, an article appeared in the Wichita Eagle and Beacon titled &#34;BTK case unsolved, 30 years later.&#34;  This article is believed to have prompted the &#34;Bill Thomas Killman&#34; letter, and was the first of five communications from BTK in 2004. The letter was postmarked exactly two months after that article was published. &#34;HL&#34; are the initials of the staff writer for the Wichita Eagle and Beacon who wrote that article; Hurst Laviana. That article is on-line at <a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/archive/7732738.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/archive/7732738.htm</a> Other possible HL&#39;s, I guess, could include any other person who is closely associated with the investigation and/or the local Wichita Media and who have H and L as initials. However, the timing and increased frequency of subsequent communications from BTK following Laviana&#39;s article suggests this message, hidden in the name of the sender and addressed to the paper that published the article, specifically identified and was intended for that article&#39;s author. <BR> <BR>What are the odds? After stumbling upon this anagram within the senders name I began to wonder. Two specific persons connected to the case are identified by their initials. One is the killer and the other a reporter whose article appears to have sparked the recent flood of communication from that killer, on a letter addressed to the reporter&#39;s employer. Within the message is the name of a photo studio which had locations just 3-5 minutes away from 4 of the 6 BTK attributed crime scenes. At least one victim&#39;s portrait was taken at their studio. The released profile of the killer indicates a knowledge of photography and access to equipment to process photographs. All of this is suggested within the space of just 17 rearranged letters of the senders name. In addition, subsequent communications from the killer contained other hidden word puzzles, and the Wichita Police have shown an interest in &#34;Games&#34; magazine&#39;s Wichita subscribers. I think it is at least plausible that this is a intentionally constructed anagram. <BR> <BR>So now we have him, right? Well, no. No matter how unlikely, this anagram may still be merely coincidental or possibly even serendipitous. Even if it is an intentional act by BTK,  it may be meant to deceive just as easily as it was meant to inform. For instance, BTK may think that the Wichita Police Department already suspects that he is/was associated in some way with photo studios generally, or even Olan Mills in particular. He then crafts this &#34;clue&#34; and stuffs the envelope with evidence that he is the genuine killer, but does not provide any real help in identifying him. If the WPD solve the &#34;Bill Thomas Killman&#34; anagram, and I believe that they have, this may be another BTK attempt to lead them down yet another dead end. After all, that same anagram can be rearranged &#34;HL, Am I Olan Mills?, BTK.&#34; Further, his intent may be nothing more than an attempt to unjustly implicate a respected business and it&#39;s employees for some past grievance,  merely as a cruel prank, or because it was convenient &#40;i.e. he could fit this anagram within the letter space of the name of a plausible person&#41;. <BR> <BR>So what&#39;s the point? I don&#39;t know, but I don&#39;t think this anagram should be ignored either. If BTK intended to provide this &#34;clue&#34; it&#39;s unlikely to point directly at him. He might have some very tangential and tantalizing close relationship which has faded almost completely over time. Maybe he delivered bottled water or photographic chemicals, or worked on their copier, or dated the receptionist, or Petra had her portrait done there. Whatever the connection, if any, it is unlikely to produce a nice clean list of suspects. Nothing so simple as merely looking at employment records. Personally, I do not believe he worked for Olan Mills, that would be much too easy and too direct.  <BR> <BR>When I first stumbled across this I was certain it was important and focused on the words in the message. After working on this for over a week, I believe the message is of less importance than the act of planting the message itself. A BTK counterintelligence move to see if it will be discovered, what importance is placed on the message, and watching how the opposition reacts. Or perhaps it is just more noise to hide any unintentionally disclosed information that might lead to his capture.  <BR>  <BR>I lived in the Wichita area during many of the events which surround this case, and like a lot of people I thought BTK was dead or in jail. Shows how smart I am! Like most Wichita residents, both past and present, I have my own favorite suspect. Well to be perfectly honest, I have two suspects. I guess there could be two perpetrators, working together or even independently copying each other. Over the years I lost track of them both. As far as I can remember neither worked for Olan Mills. Even the sticky DNA could have been planted to mislead, or even exclude the real killer if he was ever tested &#40;think &#34;Presumed Innocent&#34;&#41;. But I should leave these topics for another time. In the end I don&#39;t think BTK will ever be caught, but who knows?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
